 to  frodo and breaks the news to the  frightened hobbit that his little ring  is the one ring that sauron is avidly  pursuing the scene takes place in bag  end where gandalf calmly explains that  in the region of region the elves used  to make their own magical rings he  describes them as mere essays in the  craft compared to the rings of power but  warns that they still would have been  dangerous for mortals to wield  here's a question  where on earth do all of these powerful  rings come from and how do they end up  unevenly distributed between the various  races of middle earth well in  predictable fashion tolkien provided a  significant amount of information about  this seemingly minor detail via a story  that starts back in the second age of  middle earth at the beginning of the age  sauron takes over the role of the dark  lord from his master morgoth he keeps a  low profile for several centuries then  makes his first moves building up his  strength and choosing mordor is his  stomping ground but things don't move  quickly enough so he shapes himself into  an attractive physical appearance calls  himself anatar the lord of gifts and  pays a visit to the elves anatar  immediately tries to worm his way into  their affections by offering his very  real wisdom and skill as a craftsman one  group of elves who live in region are  more than a little appreciative of  anatar's rival and their leader keller  brimbour soaks up everything that he is  willing to teach eventually the elven  lord and his guild of craftsmen join up  with the lord of gifts and they all  begin forging a number of magic rings  while these rings would eventually end  up in the hands of men and dwarves at  this point they're solely considered  elven rings  after 16 of the rings of power are  forged sauron sneaks back into mordor  and crafts the one ring in the meantime  kala brimbore creates the three elven  rings using the techniques he had been  taught by anatar crucially however  sauron had nothing to do with their  creation and never once laid a hand on  them giving the three rings an element  of purity that isn't present in the  others even so keller bremborg couldn't  have made these without sauron's  mentorship and the silmarillion states  that even they were subject to the one  one ring to  [Music]  as soon as sauron puts on his new ring  the elves become aware of his presence  and take their rings off refusing to be  controlled by the new dark lord enraged  by his own failure sauron responds by  attacking oregon and seizing the nine  rings for himself he tortures  calibrimbore and before he dies the  elven lord reveals where seven of the  others are however he never tells sauron  where he sent the three elven rings  which remains safe and well hidden once  sauron has acquired most of the rings of  power he distributes them to the various  men and dwarves that he wants to  dominate with his will the dwarves end  up too stubborn to control but their  rings still corrupt their hearts and  drive them to become obsessed with  treasure men are much easier to sway and  the lords and kings who wield the rings  of power are turned into some of  sauron's most feared servants  sauron's greatest success is with the  nine rings which are given to a group of  mortal men the silmarillion explains the  extraordinary powers of this group of  wraiths saying that if they wanted to  they could walk unseen by anybody else  in the world and that they themselves  could see things nobody else could  as they walk in both the real world and  the wraith world these men slowly begin  to lose themselves one by one they fall  under the influence of the one ring  causing them to become permanently  invisible and driving them into total  thraldom to the power of mordor they  later become known as the nazgul these  wraiths remain sauron's fiercely loyal  servants throughout their unnaturally  long lives led by the witch king of  angmar they spread terror throughout  middle-earth right up until the moment  at which the one ring is finally  destroyed  much attention is often given to the  rings claimed by men and elves but you  don't often hear about a lot of those  wielded by the dwarves that's because  the dwarves aren't all that interested  in being dominated by anyone good or bad  in fact throughout the silmarillion they  consistently switch between the roles of  heroes and villains usually depending on  the situation that said when sauron  gives seven of the dwarf lords their own  rings of power it doesn't have quite the  effect that he hoped the silmarillion  says that the dwarves proved tough and  hard to tame and used their rings for  one purpose acquiring wealth the power  of the rings also causes the dwarves to  become obsessively possessive over their  treasures filling them with wrath and  hatred towards anybody who would  threaten them over the years these dwarf  lords grow seven hordes of treasure and  a golden ring is rumored to be at the  heart of each one sauron's influence  over the dwarves is limited however as  they can't be reduced to shadows their  wills can't be enslaved and their lives  aren't otherwise affected by the rings  even so things don't turn out  particularly well for the dwarven rings  sauron recovers three of them while the  remaining four are devoured by dragons  while this isn't quite what sauron  planned tolkien points out in the  silmarillion that the greed of gold  stirred in the hearts of these dwarves  proved to be quite useful to the dark  lord  next up you've got the three elven rings  these are special in a number of ways  first off they are the only rings of  power that are definitely forged outside  of sauron's influence he never touched  them during their creation and so they  are regarded as more pure than the  others even though the one ring is still  ultimately able to dominate them when  the one ring is lost though the elven  rings can be used for their original  purpose which is very different from the  other rings the silmarillion explains  that the three could ward off the decays  of time and postpone the weariness of  the world in this sense they are  instruments of preservation and healing  they're also by far the most magical  rings and their wielders use them to  inspire both life and hope in those who  live under them the elven rings pass  through a few owners over the years but  by the end of the third age they're kept  by three different people galadriel  guards nenia the ring of water this is  nenu the ring of adamant  and i am its keeper meanwhile elrod  keeps vilya the ring of air and gandalf  is given naria the ring of fire these  rings are often used to build havens of  peace and protection most notably  lothorian and rivendell as such sauron  greatly covets their unsullied power  even though he's never able to track  them down tolkien does make it clear  that despite their disconnect from  sauron's tampering the one ring is  powerful enough to dominate the three as  well in the lord of the rings he  explains that if the dark lord were to  get hold of the one ring all of the work  that had been done with the three would  be brought to ruin  finally the piece de resistance the one  ring this is the ultimate piece in the  middle earth jewelry box it isn't just a  ring of power like the others sauron  infuses this ring with his own will and  power to give it the strength to  dominate the others in the fellowship of  the ring the high stakes nature of the  one is laid bare in this book it's  pointed out that because he allowed a  significant portion of his power to pass  into it sauron can control any of the  others as long as he gets hold of the  one sauron needs only this ring to cover  all the lands of a second darkness  the one ring has a number of effects on  mortals too most famously it's able to  turn its wearer invisible it can also  bring them into the spiritual realm  gives them an understanding of different  languages and can extend their lives  indefinitely more than all the other  rings the one ring is also able to take  care of itself it can sense its master's  call and responds in very direct ways  doing things like changing sizes or even  abandoning its wearer the ones desired  to return to sauron is so strong in fact  that it can overcome the will of anyone  who possesses it  each of the rings of power has its own  story powerful properties and elements  of good or evil however the actual way  in which they work is something of a  mystery for one thing as you probably  know now each of them impacts their  owners in different ways take  invisibility for example neither dwarves  nor elves disappear due to the use of  their rings while men eventually become  creatures of ghostly terror and mortals  seem to vanish suddenly when they use  the one there's certainly no doubt that  the rings of power enhance the abilities  of their wares in some way or another  sometimes this is subtle such as making  it easier for the dwarves to cultivate  their wealth at other times it's very  direct like bilbo turning invisible to  fight giant spiders in mirkwood  throughout his writing about magic rings  though tolkien never seems to codify a  specific set of rules under which they  operate instead he uses their physical  abilities to hint at a much larger more  impressive and more spiritual element to  their magical powers the one theme that  is consistent across all of the rings  however is their domination and draw  toward the one ring this is even true  for the safer elven rings meanwhile the  rings of the men and dwarves are  outright cursed and perverted by sauron  the silmarillion simply states that in  the end all of the rings betrayed those  who used them while gandalf points out  in the fellowship of the ring that  anybody who wields a great ring will be  devoured by the dark power sooner or  later  [Music]  you
 

